# LDAPS auth — integration notes

Search-then-bind LDAPS authentication with a bounded connection pool and group
extraction. Works against Active Directory and POSIX/OpenLDAP out of the box.

## Dependency

    npm install ldapts

(zod is already in the scaffold.)

## Env vars

Required: LDAP_URL, LDAP_BIND_DN, LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD, LDAP_BASE_DN.
Optional: LDAP_USER_FILTER ({{username}} placeholder), LDAP_GROUP_ATTR
(default memberOf), LDAP_CA_CERT_PATH (PEM bundle for a private CA),
LDAP_TLS_REJECT_UNAUTHORIZED (default true — only ever 'false' in a lab),
LDAP_POOL_SIZE (default 4), LDAP_TIMEOUT_MS (default 5000).

Declare them in the project's mock2.yaml/env handling like any other secret —
never hardcode the bind password.

## Glue (the only code you write)

    import { authenticate, userInGroup, closeLdaps } from './lib/ldaps/auth.js';

    app.post('/api/login', async (req, res) => {
      const { username, password } = req.body ?? {};
      const user = await authenticate(String(username ?? ''), String(password ?? ''));
      if (!user) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid credentials' });
      if (!userInGroup(user, 'app-users')) return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Not authorized' });
      // issue your session/JWT here from user.dn / user.email / user.groups
      res.json({ ok: true, user: { name: user.displayName, email: user.email } });
    });

    process.on('SIGTERM', () => { void closeLdaps(); });

## Behavior notes

- authenticate() returns null for BOTH unknown user and wrong password — do not
  differentiate in the response.
- Empty passwords are rejected before any bind (an empty password would be an
  anonymous bind that "succeeds" on many servers).
- User input is RFC 4515-escaped before it reaches a search filter.
- The pool is bounded (LDAP_POOL_SIZE); broken connections are discarded and
  recreated. Call closeLdaps() on shutdown.

